-2.3 The same construction has the relative parameterized form needed for a whole isotopy. Write its cores as $h_t:C\to X$ and its supplied framing annuli as $E_t$. If $h'_t$ is sufficiently $C^1$-close to $h_t$ and equal to it on fixed endpoint collars, set $h_{r,t}=h_t+r(h'_t-h_t)$ for $0\le r,t\le1$ in the ambient $\mathbb R^3$ coordinates. Compactness of $C\times[0,1]$ gives uniform immersion bounds, separation of distant source points, and clearance from $\partial X$; consequently all $h_{r,t}$ are embeddings in $X$ for a sufficiently small perturbation. The trace $(x,r,t)\mapsto(h_{r,t}(x),r,t)$ is embedded. In finitely many spatial normal-coordinate charts along this trace extend $\partial_rh_{r,t}$ constantly in the spatial normal coordinates, and use smooth cutoffs and a finite partition of unity to obtain a vector field $V_{r,t}(y)$, smooth jointly in all variables, with one fixed compact support in $X$ and value $\partial_rh_{r,t}(x)$ at $y=h_{r,t}(x)$. The charts can be taken on slightly extended parameter intervals, so the construction is smooth at parameter faces as well. On the $t$-endpoint collars this velocity is zero; multiply the extension by a $t$-cutoff zero on smaller such collars and one outside the original collars. This preserves its prescribed core values and makes $V_{r,t}=0$ on the smaller collars. Integrate in the auxiliary variable $r$, with initial condition $\Psi_{0,t}=\mathrm{id}$. Compact support gives existence for the full interval, and smooth parameter dependence and uniqueness give $\Psi_{r,t}h_t=h_{r,t}$. Each $\Psi_{r,t}$ is an ambient diffeomorphism; it is the identity for $t$ near either endpoint. Therefore $E'_t=\Psi_{1,t}(E_t)$ is a smooth family of embedded framing annuli over $h'_t$ agreeing exactly with the supplied framed family on endpoint collars. If $E_t=G_t(E_0)$ is the supplied ambient framed isotopy, then $\Psi_{1,t}\circ G_t$ is itself an ambient isotopy realizing this transported family, with the same endpoint maps. For each $r$, the analogous family $\Psi_{r,t}(E_t)$ is framed, so this is a deformation of the whole original framed isotopy relative its endpoints, not merely a lift of one perturbed slice. No full twist is inserted at an endpoint. [step 2.2, given, construct]
